     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Full Roster: The Standard Model Chart",
     "content": "Here it is — the chart itself. Take a moment; you already know every piece.\n\n**The 6 quarks** (top two rows): **up, down** / **charm, strange** / **top, bottom.** They carry fractional electric charge and the strong-force property called *color*, so they never appear alone — they bind into protons, neutrons, and other composites.\n\n**The 6 leptons** (bottom two rows): the **electron, muon, tau** — each carrying charge −1 — and their three partner **neutrinos** (electron-neutrino, muon-neutrino, tau-neutrino), which are electrically neutral and almost weightless.\n\nNotice the columns. The chart is arranged in **three generations** — three vertical families, each a heavier echo of the one before. Generation I (up, down, electron, electron-neutrino) builds everything around you. Generations II and III are heavier copies that decay in fractions of a second.\n\n**The force carriers** (right block): the **gluon** (strong force — really 8 of them), the **photon** (electromagnetism), and the **W and Z** (weak force).\n\n**The Higgs** stands apart — the particle of the field that gives the others their mass.\n\nCount the distinct types: 6 + 6 = 12 matter particles, plus 4 force-carrier types (gluon, photon, W, Z), plus the Higgs = **17 elementary particle types.** That's the entire known inventory of the fundamental universe.",
